Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: One medium throughout — contemporary live-action, working-class Milwaukee in winter, naturalistic and grainy. Dream passages use the same live-action medium in warmer, softer light.

FADE IN:

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A narrow room in a fifth-floor walk-up. Peeling paint, a mattress on a metal frame, a library book face-down on the blanket. THE NARRATOR — late twenties, thick through the shoulders, flour dried under his fingernails, gray hoodie — sits on the edge of the bed and talks straight to us.

THE NARRATOR
Big brother was mine. I'm not talking about that stupid book we had to read in junior high, about how the world was gonna turn weird and everybody could see everything you were doing all the time. No. I'm talking about my real Big Brother.

He glances at the closed door. Lowers his voice.

THE NARRATOR
The reason why I'm telling you all this stuff is because something weird is happening to me. I can't really tell Nick, or Ma, or Bobby down at the pizza joint, because they wouldn't understand. Hell, I don't understand what's happening.

EXT. KIRK STREET SIDEWALK - DAY

A big brick apartment building. Gray slush at the curb. NICK — early thirties, huge, reddish-brown hair he clearly never combed, a scar on his chin and another split through his left eyebrow, work boots, open canvas coat — carries six loaded grocery bags in two hands like they're empty. THE NARRATOR follows with the other two.

At the curb sits a 1980 Pontiac Trans Am: burnt orange, yellow flames down the side, big eagle on the hood, T-top.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
His face looked like a

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR — mid-twenties, thin through the shoulders, flour dried under his fingernails, gray hoodie — sits on the edge of the bed and talks straight to us.

THE NARRATOR
One time, back when he was in high school, I remember Nick getting so pissed off at this one kid named Steve. Steve said something mean to Nick's girlfriend, and she came to him crying about it. Nick was so mad.

EXT. STEVE'S FRONT YARD - NIGHT

Flashback. A dark suburban lawn, chain-link fence, one porch light. NICK at seventeen — already huge, the same uncombed reddish-brown hair — walks the grass shaking a can of lighter fluid.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He went over to that kid's house in the middle of the night, took a can of lighter fluid, and sprayed it around his front yard a little bit, then he set that crap on fire.

A match drops. Fire runs across the wet grass in a low blue line. Nick's face goes orange. He doesn't move.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And he got away with it too. One of his friends was there and saw him do it, but everybody is afraid of Nick, so the guy said nothing. What a set of balls.

A friend's silhouette backs away behind the fence. Nick watches the flames like they're television.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I am usually proud that Nick is my brother. But the question is, would I want Sionna to meet Nick? He is so off the handle, and she has herself together so well. He wouldn't even understand her.

EXT. PARK PATH - DAY

Late fall. Maples burning orange and red over a cracked asphalt path. THE NARRATOR walks with SIONNA — mid-twenties, slim, long dark hair in a loose braid, pale ice-blue eyes, gray knit hat, secondhand wool coat, a rolled purple yoga mat under one arm. He carries a borrowed mat, holding it wrong.

SIONNA
The sugars get caught in the leaves. It's like the trees are shutting down for the winter — the sugars get caught, and it makes them turn these spectacular colors.

He stops. Picks up a leaf. Turns it over.

THE NARRATOR
Sometimes when people get caught in bad situations, they change into something better. Just like leaves.

She looks at him a long moment.

SIONNA
That's beautiful.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I never had anyone tell me that something I said was beautiful before.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - STACKS - DAY

Fluorescent light, worn carpet. THE NARRATOR pulls a paperback on yoga off the exercise shelf. He turns the corner and finds a whole shelf of world religion. He takes down a book on Buddhism.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This part I read was about attachment. How attaching ourselves to other people or things will make us miserable and cause suffering.

He sits on the floor between the stacks, back against the shelf, reading. Outside the high window, a bus hisses past.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
We look for happiness in the outside world, when the way we think about things is what makes us happy. The more people have, the more they want. It said that all things are really one thing. There is no separation to begin with.

He looks up from the page at nothing at all.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It said Buddhism isn't about believing something. It's about experiencing it. I had to talk to Sionna about it.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - EVENING

A cramped kitchen, avocado linoleum, a phone on the wall with a stretched cord. THE NARRATOR leans against the fridge, library books under his arm.

THE NARRATOR
I wish you'd finally come over to my place. Even meet my Ma.

SIONNA (V.O.)
(on the phone)
Are you sure? It's kind of a big deal to meet your mother.

THE NARRATOR
Hell, she probably won't even remember meeting you when she wakes up the next morning. She's so forgetful now.

He shifts the books, gets one open on the counter.

THE NARRATOR
What I really need to talk to you about is some stuff I read in a book about Buddhism.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Oh. I didn't know you were interested in Buddhism.

THE NARRATOR
Remember when we were talking about meditating? I think I want to try it sometime.

SIONNA (V.O.)
You've already tried it, silly. You're meditating at the end of every yoga practice. You know, when we just lie there at the end — in Savasana. Corpse pose.

He laughs at himself, hangs up the phone, and carries the books into the living room.

MA sits in the overstuffed brown chair — late sixties, small and thin, gray hair pinned back, tired brown eyes, faded blue housecoat and slippers. A game show blares. She looks up at him strangely, like a stranger walked in.

THE NARRATOR
How's it goin', Ma?

MA
What? Oh. It's you.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay, Ma?

MA
Oh, I don't know. It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest. It's so strange, the feeling I have right now. I'm a little dizzy too.

Her hand goes flat against her housecoat. He looks at the wall phone.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - WAITING ROOM AND EXAM ROOM - DAY

THE NARRATOR sits in a plastic chair by the window. Gray sky, clouds hanging low, people going by on the sidewalk. A siren somewhere off in the distance. He's gone somewhere behind his eyes.

A nurse leans in and says his name. He almost jumps out of the chair.

In the exam room, DR. CARSON — fifties, silver hair, wire glasses, white coat — comes toward him with a chart.

DR. CARSON
Hello, I'm Dr. Carson. I think I may have met you before. You're the younger son, right?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. Uh — how's she doin', Doc?

DR. CARSON
Your mother's fine. She's settled in another room. The thing is, I want to talk to you about her blood pressure.

THE NARRATOR
Her blood pressure? She's been takin' her pills. I know it, I make sure she takes them religiously.

Dr. Carson looks him straight in the eye.

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. It's so high I'm afraid of the risk of a stroke. My advice is that you take her directly to the emergency room and we admit her for observation.

THE NARRATOR
Whoa, man. I can't believe this. Are you sure it's that bad?

DR. CARSON
She's been confused. And she had chest pressure this afternoon?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. She hasn't been actin' too normal lately.

Dr. Carson writes. THE NARRATOR turns toward the waiting-room phone.

Later. The doors bang open. NICK fills the waiting room, face beet red, canvas coat open over work clothes.

NICK
What the hell is this all about now?

MA is wheeled out, one hand pressed to her chest, calm as Sunday.

MA
There's a ringing in my ears. Someone must be talking about me.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's right, Ma. We're all talking about you.

INT. HOSPITAL - MA'S ROOM - DAY

MA dozes in a raised bed, IV line taped to the back of her hand, monitor ticking. THE NARRATOR sits in a vinyl chair with his work shirt folded on his knees.

SIONNA comes in with two wrapped sandwiches and the purple mat still under her arm. She hands him one. They eat by the window and talk low so Ma can sleep.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Her eyes sparkled like diamonds. I know how that sounds, but it's true. She made me feel excited and calm at the same time. Actually, I think I loved her. I just hadn't told her yet.

She squeezes his hand and goes. The door sighs shut. The monitor ticks. His head tips back against the vinyl. His eyes close.

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - DAY

Dream. Smoke, churned mud, a sky the color of ash. Men in leather and rough wool scream and fall. Blades of shining steel thrust into bodies.

THE NARRATOR stands in a soldier's filthy tunic, a short sword slack in his fist, exhausted, sick with it. At his boots lies THE DYING SOLDIER, bearded, gut-opened.

THE DYING SOLDIER
Save her.

Blood bubbles out of his mouth. His hand falls open.

THE NARRATOR
Who? Save who?

No answer. THE NARRATOR turns slowly to his left. Through dozens of men falling, NICK comes running at him — the same red hair, the same split eyebrow, a long knife low at his side.

THE NARRATOR
Nick! Nick! Thank God you're here! I thought I was going to die.

NICK
(hissing)
That you are, little brother.

The knife goes into THE NARRATOR's chest to the hilt. Nick's face is very close. Almost kind.

INT. HOSPITAL - MA'S ROOM - DAY

THE NARRATOR jerks awake gasping, both hands on his own chest. The monitor ticks. MA sleeps on, mouth open.

He sits forward, elbows on knees, breathing hard.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would Nick want to kill me? Me. His little brother. It took me fifteen minutes to tell myself it was only a dream. Still, all day I had that feeling. Betrayal by your closest family — that has to be the worst betrayal of all.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MA'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Three days later. A small dim bedroom, crucifix over the headboard. MA is tucked in, blankets to her chin. THE NARRATOR sets a blue plastic pill container on the nightstand and opens the lid.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sionna went with me to the drug store. Sunday through Saturday. Blood pressure pills, and the little Nitro ones in case of chest pain.

He turns the container so Ma can see the compartments.

THE NARRATOR
See, Ma, right here is every day of the week, and every day's got the pills in it you need. There's even separations for morning and night.

MA
(eyeing it suspiciously)
Oh my Lord. That looks a little overwhelming.

THE NARRATOR
No worries, Ma. I'm goin' to be here to make sure everything's okay, and make sure you get the right pill at the right time.

She looks up at him with those tired brown eyes.

MA
You're such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me. What would I do without you two?

He smooths her blanket. Says nothing.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
You two.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR carries the blue container out to the coffee table. The front door opens and NICK comes in, face beet red, hair helter-skelter around his pox-marked face, eyes wild.

NICK
What's up, little Bro? You got Ma all tucked in? Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

He laughs loud enough to wake the dead.

THE NARRATOR
Knock it off, man. Somebody has to make sure she stays on track. Where have you been?

NICK
Don't start giving ME shit, Bro. Where the hell do you think? I was at work. More than you can say for your sorry ass down at that bullshit pizza parlor.

THE NARRATOR
It's eight o'clock at night, man. Ma's been home like four hours.

NICK
All right, look. I'm not giving you hell. Just what is the deal around here? Are you going to keep her from strokin' out, or what?

He drops into the overstuffed brown chair, boots up on the footstool. THE NARRATOR hands him the blue container.

THE NARRATOR
I got her meds all in the same spot. Told her I'd help her keep track. Check it out.

NICK
Oh, holy shit! Aren't you nurse Nancy. Well, well. How did you come up with this little idea?

THE NARRATOR
If you must know, Sionna helped me figure it out. She's really smart and good at that stuff. You know I never could think things through.

NICK
(mocking)
If you must know. If you must know. Whatever. This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger. I mean, if you must know.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it, Nick! You haven't even met her. I don't understand how you can't like someone you don't even know, man. Give her a break!

Nick stands. He fills the room like a red Viking.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

THE NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard!

NICK
How the hell do you know? Maybe you are. You sure are a giant pussy — and that makes you and me way different, Bro.

THE NARRATOR's fists close tight.

THE NARRATOR
You're so Goddamn hateful. What got into you? Was Ma on crack or somethin' when she was pregnant with you?

Whack. Nick's open hand cracks across the side of his head. THE NARRATOR staggers into the wall, ear ringing, suddenly sick.

NICK
Don't you EVER talk that way about MY mother, you little fuck.

Nick spins out. The door slams hard enough to rattle the window glass. The blue pill container lies on the floor.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR sits on the edge of the bed, one palm cupped over his left ear, talking to us.

THE NARRATOR
I never had a screaming fight with Nick before. Hell, I never had the nerve to. It just came bubbling out of me like a box of firecrackers exploding out of my mouth.

He picks up the library book on Buddhism from the blanket. Sets it down again.

THE NARRATOR
It's okay anyway. Who needs Nick, really? Me and Ma and Sionna would be just fine without him. And in that split second, I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MA'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning light through a yellowed shade. MA lies propped on two pillows, thin, hollow-cheeked, gray at the temples, a faded pink housecoat buttoned to the throat. THE NARRATOR - thirteen, skinny, cowlick, sneakers with no laces - comes in with the blue pill container and a glass of water.

He sets the glass on the nightstand and shakes two pills into her palm.

MA
Where's your brother?

THE NARRATOR
Out.

She takes the pills, swallows, and looks at him a long moment. His left ear is red as a slap.

MA
What happened to your ear?

THE NARRATOR
I fell off the bed.

She doesn't believe him. She's too tired to say so. She squeezes his hand instead.

MA
You're a good boy.

THE NARRATOR pulls the blanket up over her shoulders and waits until her eyes close.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

The room is small, brown, and neat only where THE NARRATOR has made it neat. He carries the water glass to the sink, then stops.

Nick's boots are gone from beside the radiator. So is his jacket off the chair. The ashtray is full.

THE NARRATOR
(quiet, to us)
Nick didn't come home. In our house, that counted as a good night.

He empties the ashtray, wipes the table with his sleeve, and lines up the blue pill container square in the middle of it, where Ma can see it from the hall.

EXT. KIRK STREET STOOP - DAY

SIONNA sits on the top step - the narrator's age, dark braid over one shoulder, scabbed knees, a paperback bent open on her lap. THE NARRATOR comes out and sits one step below her, keeping his sore ear turned away.

They pass the book back and forth and talk of nothing in particular.

SIONNA
You okay?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah.

She looks at him sideways. He looks straight down the street.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR sits on the edge of the bed in the dark, the library book on Buddhism closed on his knees, talking to us. His ear still burns.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna asked me if I was okay and I said yeah, because that's what you say. I didn't tell her about Nick. I didn't tell her we had a Nick.

He lies back, one hand cupped over his ear, staring at the ceiling.

THE NARRATOR
Ma was sick, Nick was gone, and Sionna didn't know a thing about either one. That was the closest I ever came to a peaceful life.

Downstairs, a door bangs. Heavy boots start up the stairs. THE NARRATOR goes very still.

EXT. SCHOOL PLAYGROUND - DAY

Lilac bushes bloom at the back fence. The last week of fourth grade, years ago. YOUNG NARRATOR - nine, buzzed haircut, striped tee, sneakers - is folded over at the waist beside BOBBY SWENSON - nine, freckles, glasses taped at the bridge. Both are laughing so hard they can barely stand.

YOUNG NARRATOR
You HAVE to learn some responsibility!

BOBBY SWENSON
No, YOU have to learn some responsibility!

They collapse against each other. Two girls with braids and red bows watch with folded arms, then march off toward the building.

YOUNG NICK - twelve, a head taller than anyone else on the blacktop, hair in a devilish mess, denim jacket - saunters up.

YOUNG NICK
What are you two numbskulls doin'?

Bobby goes silent, staring at his shoes.

YOUNG NARRATOR
Oh, just the usual crap from Mrs. Stark. She thinks we need to learn some responsibility.

He breaks up laughing again. Young Nick slaps his shoulder.

YOUNG NICK
Little Bro, you better start listening, man. Ma is pissed at you already. Don't get sent to the principal's office.

MRS. STARK strides across the blacktop - stout, tight gray curls, glasses swinging from a beaded chain.

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olsen! Mr. Olsen! Would you like to explain yourself to me before I send you in to talk to Mr. Pauls?

YOUNG NICK
Um, sorry Mrs. Stark. He didn't mean anything by it. He and Bobby were just goofing around. I'll talk to him and Ma will talk to him. He'll never act up in class again. I guarantee it.

She puts on her glasses, tips her head back, takes Young Nick in head to toe.

MRS. STARK
Well, Nick, I know you're a good student. It's too bad your brother here doesn't have the same level head as you. I guess I'll let it pass this time.

She turns on the boys.

MRS. STARK
YOU, young man, need to follow your brother's example and start paying attention.

She turns on her heels and marches back to the school. Bobby exhales.

YOUNG NICK
You two knock it off. That is the last time I'm getting involved in your stupid crap.

He musses the hair on top of Young Narrator's head.

YOUNG NICK
I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

YOUNG NARRATOR
I won't Nick. I won't.

Young Nick walks off toward three other sixth-graders, who crowd in to hear the story. He spreads his arms like a king.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made me love him. It made me hate him. If they only knew the kind of stuff he did.

The boys on the blacktop laugh at whatever Nick is telling them.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like Nick lived in a bubble, and no one could touch him. It was like he was magic or something.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

THE NARRATOR - late twenties, flour on a stained apron, rag in hand - stares out the front window at green grass and warm light. A community college English syllabus is folded beside the order pad.

THE NARRATOR
He had always been the alpha dog of the family. Well, actually, the alpha dog of any group he was ever a part of.

He wipes the same spot on the table twice.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna hasn't met Nick yet. So far, I've managed to keep them apart. But sooner or later, they'll end up in the same place.

He sets the rag down.

THE NARRATOR
They're both the Alpha and the Omega, the beginning and the end - of me anyway. Without both of them, who would I be?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

MA sits in her chair before the television, a cop show flickering, the blue pill container on the side table. NICK comes in - big boots, brown suede jacket - and throws his keys into the basket by the door. He bends to kiss her cheek.

NICK
Those assholes next to me in the line almost caught me in the goddamn drill press today.

MA
Nick, do you have to swear? It's so unlike you.

NICK
Okay, okay, Ma. I won't swear in front of ya; don't want to hurt your pretty ears.

She smiles her hesitant smile and turns back to the screen. THE NARRATOR comes in from the hall with a schoolbook under his arm.

NICK
So, what's up with you?

THE NARRATOR
Ah, not much. Just working and thinking about going to school.

Nick stops. He stares at him as if he'd been told Ma was pregnant.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for?

THE NARRATOR
Community college. A business degree. Maybe I can manage a restaurant someplace.

NICK
How the hell are you going to have time to watch Ma if you're screwing around with those pencil-pushers?

THE NARRATOR
Sionna told me I could qualify for a low-interest student loan. I checked it all out, and applied, and they gave me the money.

NICK
Whoa, whoa, whoa, dude. I can't believe you are letting this chick tell you what to do. It's not like she's gonna be around to help pay this shit back! And it sure ain't gonna be me helping your sorry ass out.

THE NARRATOR
I don't want to work in a pizza place my whole life. And I don't do everything Sionna tells me - this was my idea. It would really mean a lot to me if you could just step up and be on my side for once. You are my brother, man.

NICK
Be on your side? Be on your side? Man, do you actually remember your own life? How many times have I saved your ass? Can you keep count?

Ma looks up, forehead creased, and shakes her head slowly - please don't argue, boys. She folds her hands in her lap and turns back to the television.

THE NARRATOR
I'll be here for Ma. Classes aren't all day.

Nick leans in, lowering his voice so Ma can't hear. Suddenly delighted.

NICK
Do you remember Donny Amstel? Pushing you around in the locker room? I followed him home from school. Shoved my body in his front door so he couldn't close it. He looked like he was about to shit his pants.

He giggles like a little kid.

NICK
I told him if he ever threatened you again he would be killed. I told him our family had mafia connections. And he believed me! It worked though, right?

THE NARRATOR
It did work, Nick. It did. You are something else, man.

Nick hits him lightly on the arm.

NICK
You're not just my brother, man. You're my best friend.

The narrator, caught off guard, almost smiles. Nick's grin thins out.

NICK
Just remember this, Bro. Nothin', I mean nothing is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl either. Bro's before ho's. You know what I mean?

A long beat. The narrator keeps his face very still.

THE NARRATOR
Sure, Nick. Sure. I get you, man.

EXT. KIRK STREET SIDEWALK - DAY

Blue sky, warm air. THE NARRATOR and SIONNA - dark braid, scabbed knees traded for jeans and a thin cardigan - walk together past the hardware store, where three boys on skateboards look them over and burst out laughing.

THE NARRATOR
What the hell is so funny about us?

SIONNA
Nothing, unless you're thirteen. Thirteen year olds think everything is funny.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like I want to go over there and punch that kid in the face, and you're brushing it off like it's nothing.

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world. You aren't stupid, or violent, or useless. You know more than they do. You have a purpose.

THE NARRATOR
I know that logically. It still pisses me off.

SIONNA
That's what loving kindness is. The ability to open our hearts and accept everyone - even those kids on the corner laughing at us. It might take my whole life, but I'm bound and determined to bring it out of me.

THE NARRATOR
Let me know if you do, because if my girlfriend is loving everyone, I think I'm going to need a new one.

She laughs. She stops walking and looks up at him.

SIONNA
Love is the universal language, and we know how to speak it.

THE NARRATOR
You're the only one I can speak it to, I guess.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

He goes still. Then he leans down and kisses her. The street noise falls away.

THE NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

They walk on.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A low couch, beer cans, a ceramic pipe going around. NICK sprawls at the center, face beet red, smoke sputtering out of the corner of his mouth. BOB - long hair, flannel shirt - and SAMMY - wiry, mustache, Brewers cap - flank him. THE NARRATOR leans back in a rocking chair, a Bud untouched in his hand.

BOB
I don't know about you guys, but I'm needin' some new action. This girlfriend of mine used to be a freak, but she won't put out anymore.

SAMMY
Same old story, man. They just want to get you trapped, and then start spending your cash.

NICK
There ain't many good looking girls in Milwaukee, period. Ain't that right, Bro? Oh, wait - never mind. He's got the perfect girlfriend. She has him wrapped right now.

SAMMY
Man, do you need a "get out of jail free" card or something?

They howl. The narrator rocks slowly, smiling.

THE NARRATOR
You three wouldn't recognize class and intelligence if it jumped up and bit your ass.

More laughing. Nick raises his glass to him without looking at him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick pretends to be all understanding when we're alone, then calls me out in front of these guys. All that "best friend" crap just doesn't hold water.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - BARROOM - NIGHT

Heavy rock, smoke, packed tables. Quarters stacked on the rail of the pool table in the next room. NICK cuts through the crowd and people scooch over for him. He orders a Jack and Coke. THE NARRATOR takes a Bud and blinks at the smoke stinging his eyes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hadn't been in here for six months. It seemed strange, as if I were an alien visiting the planet. All of a sudden it felt so - dumb. Just dumb.

TONYA, a waitress in blue jeans and a white blouse, sails past with a tray. Sammy elbows Nick and nods at her; she catches the look and frowns.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
When in Rome, do as the Romans do. I took a deep breath.

Nick sets down his tumbler, staring past the doorway into the pool room.

NICK
Hold on a second. That looks like Lindsey over there.

THE NARRATOR
Why does it matter, Bro? You broke up with her - remember?

NICK
I just keep tabs on her. No one told me she was seeing anyone.

THE NARRATOR
Drop it, man. It didn't work out. She can see whoever she wants.

NICK
Kiss my ass! Did I ask your opinion?

Nick shoves off the bar. Bob and Sammy exchange a look. So does the narrator - the oh shit look - and they follow.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - POOL ROOM - NIGHT

LINDSEY - light blonde hair, brown eyes, pink sweater and boots - stands at the near table with RAFAEL, black wavy hair, dark eyes. NICK walks straight up.

NICK
What's up, Lindsey?

LINDSEY
Oh, hi, Nick. Not much.

Rafael puts an arm around her, protective. His brows dip down the midline of his face.

RAFAEL
Who is this?

LINDSEY
Oh, just an old friend.

NICK
Where did you meet this one? I didn't hear of you dating anybody. We keep our ears out, if you know what I mean.

LINDSEY
Nick, why does it matter to you?

Players lean on their cues. The room goes quiet under the music.

THE NARRATOR
Nick - leave it alone.

He tries to step in front of his brother. Nick shoves him aside.

RAFAEL
What the hell is wrong with you? I never even seen you before, and you're in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's nostrils flare. His fists close.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, Nick, listen - you don't have any problem with this guy. You don't even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it, man, just leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! It's none of your Goddamn business. You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face, asshole.

Lindsey peels away from Rafael and flattens against the wall. A waitress backs out of the room toward the kitchen.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it! Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick's head turns. His mouth twists into a shape the narrator has never seen aimed at him. The right fist draws back.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like everything was in slow motion. I had plenty of time, because time was standing still.

The fist comes. The narrator's head swerves back; he catches himself on his right foot. Knuckles graze air.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
At that moment I knew what I was truly afraid of. I was afraid of being alone.

JOE, the owner, and DUTCH, his bouncer, crash into the room and pin Nick's arms.

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

Nick head-butts Dutch and rips free. Glass goes over. Lindsey drags Rafael toward the door. Bob and Sammy grab at Nick, shouting.

The narrator turns and runs.

EXT. SIONNA'S FRONT PORCH - NIGHT

He comes up the street at a dead run, jumping the curb, chest heaving, breath white in the cold. He hammers on the door.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
My ears were reverberating with the sound of my own heartbeat. I thought I was running home.

The porch light comes on. SIONNA opens the door. He steps into her arms.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A green suede sofa, a low lamp, incense burned down to ash. THE NARRATOR sits forward, hands shaking. SIONNA listens without moving.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR
Yes, he did.

SIONNA
No one does that, you know. I mean, no one who really cares about you.

THE NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can't explain Nick. At this rate, you may never meet him. I don't know if we'll ever even talk to each other again.

SIONNA
He is like a thorn in your side. He obviously has issues with his self-esteem. I think he is a controlling ass, and you are showing him that you don't need him. You are the voice of reason, and he doesn't understand reason.

THE NARRATOR
I love Nick, though. He's my brother. He always took care of us - me and Ma.

She strokes the side of his face.

SIONNA
Look at all the progress you have made that he hasn't. You can't let him intimidate you, or stop you from your purpose.

THE NARRATOR
My purpose. What is my purpose?

SIONNA
I don't know. I suppose everyone's purpose is different. My thought is that we really won't know until life is over.

THE NARRATOR
Um, isn't that a little too late?

He starts laughing. So does she. Then the stillness comes back.

THE NARRATOR
I have to go check on Ma.

SIONNA
Don't you think that is exactly where Nick will go? He is out of control right now. Your Ma is okay for one night. Stay with me.

She sits back on the sofa. He lies down and rests his head in her lap. She massages his temples.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna, the first time I met you, I felt so comfortable. It was like I had finally come home.

She leans down and kisses the top of his head. Her eyes shine.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

His eyes begin to close.

SIONNA
By the way - the purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it. To be the best person you can while you're here.

THE NARRATOR
I hope I can.

EXT. DREAM LAKESHORE - DAY

The world drains to black and white. THE NARRATOR stands in sand at the edge of a still lake, the same man - but in folded leather boots to the knee, dirty stockings, a tunic smeared with mud and worse. He sways, exhausted, mouth dry.

He wades into the reeds, kneels, cups water in both hands and drinks.

The wind begins to howl. The water ripples, then laps in waves over his boots. Nearby limbs bend as if a great force were on them.

From the middle of the lake, SIONNA rises - long brown hair, pale blue eyes, white fabric swirling from her gown, wind and water turning around her in slow motion.

She lifts her arms like a conductor before a symphony. Her long sleeves slice through the water.

She is completely dry.

EXT. DREAM LAKESHORE - DAY

Color floods the world - vibrant greens, blues, yellows. THE NARRATOR wakes half out of his stupor. SIONNA walks effortlessly through the clear blue water, nearer now, her gown dry, her long brown hair lifting. He holds his arms out to her. They embrace.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
A feeling of total and complete serenity enveloped me. Then, without warning, alarm was there.

The air goes dead still. Both their faces change.

From the woods to their right, NICK emerges - a giant, huge teeth, eyes of fire, whirling a chained mace above his head. His footsteps shake the earth. The chains clang and rattle.

THE NARRATOR steps in front of SIONNA. She spreads her arms, sleeves blowing out until she seems to fly.

NICK is shoved backward by an invisible force - water against fire. His face goes wild and furious; the mace will not obey his body. He shrinks to normal size.

The wind stops. NICK vanishes into thin air.

SIONNA turns and smiles. THE NARRATOR strokes her hair, kisses her face.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
They were real lips. It was real hair.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR bolts upright off the couch, nearly slamming SIONNA'S jaw with his head. They are folded together, disoriented.

THE NARRATOR
What the -

SIONNA
Oh my gosh! What's going on? We must have drifted off to sleep.

She rubs the sleep out of her eyes. He squints at the kitchen clock: three a.m.

THE NARRATOR
I had the craziest dream. You were an angel, and came out of the water. You protected us from Nick with some kind of magical force!

She giggles, eyes already closing again.

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right. Let's go to bed and get some sleep. We've had an interesting day.

THE NARRATOR
You can say that again.

They shuffle down the dark hallway together.

INT. MA'S LIVING ROOM - EVENING

MA sits in her chair, little afghan across her lap, the squawk-box murmuring. SIONNA sets down a steaming casserole - butternut squash, cheese, crunchy nuts. MA tastes it, surprised, and nods.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The next months dragged on. School, work, Ma, Sionna, homework - all of it pulling the last of my energy.

SIONNA thumbs pills into a weekly container, checking each slot. THE NARRATOR watches from the doorway, dead on his feet.

SIONNA sits beside MA and reads aloud to her. MA listens carefully, arms folded in her lap.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Within an hour she'd forget everything we read. It didn't matter. She remembered who said it, and the feeling behind it.

MA
Why doesn't Nick ever come around? Did I do something?

THE NARRATOR
He's got a poker game, Ma. Or the gym. You know Nick.

MA nods, believing him. THE NARRATOR looks away.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She had no idea about the bar, or that he tried to punch me out. It would have shocked her into a heart attack. She was starting to see me as the leader of our little family.

INT. NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - DAY

Textbooks, a legal pad, a scrawled line: I THINK, THEREFORE I AM. THE NARRATOR chews his pen. The doorbell rings. He returns with SIONNA - hair pinned up on top of her head, sandals, flowered shirt. She flops onto the bed with an exhausted sigh.

SIONNA
Geez, what a day. I have been running around like a chicken with its head cut off. Yoga, the store, notes for comparative religion, the library, and now here.

He watches her mouth move, dreamy.

THE NARRATOR
I love you.

SIONNA
(squinting, suspicious)
Have you been listening to anything I said?

THE NARRATOR
Every word.

SIONNA
So, what's your paper about?

THE NARRATOR
It's not a big paper. Just some thoughts on dualism and Descartes.

SIONNA
Perception is so cool to think about. Like, is this bed here when you don't see it?

THE NARRATOR
Does a tree that falls in the forest make a sound if no one hears it? Right, that kind of shit.

SIONNA
Do I exist if you aren't seeing me? And if I don't - does it matter what I do?

THE NARRATOR
Don't fuck with my head. I can't take it anymore.

She cradles his head in her hands.

SIONNA
You know my parents were hippies, right?

THE NARRATOR
No, you never told me that.

SIONNA
I was born in 1970. My mom always said she got pregnant with me at Woodstock. My dad went by "Mushroom," my mom went by "Jasmine." I mean, how do you think I got the name Sionna?

THE NARRATOR
I always wondered. I've never heard that name before.

SIONNA
Sionna is a Celtic goddess. It's really pronounced like "Shanna," but nobody would get that, so they said it the way it's spelled. She was the Matron of the river Shannon - possessor of wisdom, because the river was one of the seven rivers of knowledge springing from the Celtic underworld of the dead.

THE NARRATOR
Wow. Your mom and dad put some thought into that one.

SIONNA
My mom and dad smoked a lot of pot and dropped a lot of acid. I'm surprised my brain works at all.

THE NARRATOR
So, have you ever done drugs?

SIONNA
I probably smoked more secondhand pot smoke before I was three than most adults do on purpose. Why do you ask?

THE NARRATOR
I was always under Nick's watch. It was fine for him to do anything, but catch me, and watch out. Can you get some? We could hang out at your house and get really stoned and see what happens.

SIONNA
(laughing)
I know lots of people at school that can hook me up. It's not a big deal. It'll probably be legalized in a few years anyway - the government doesn't give a crap about health, they just want to tax things.

She swings off the bed and kisses his forehead.

SIONNA
Okay, hun. I'll do your dirty work and get in touch with you later. I have study group in a half hour.

EXT. SIDEWALK OUTSIDE THE PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Empty park across the street. THE NARRATOR comes out of the little restaurant with a large black coffee to go, turns - and nearly walks into KEVIN MCCLEARY: short hair gelled and parted crisply, hulking arms, quads pulling tight on his pant legs.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Hey man, what's up? Haven't seen you much down at the gym. What's goin' on?

THE NARRATOR
Hi, Kev. Just haven't had the time. Taking some classes, still working.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Man, I heard what happened at Joe's. What the hell? I heard you stopped Nick from beating some guy's ass. They said you hauled off and clocked Nick. They said you had some pretty big balls! Standin' up to Nick? That is the shit. Everybody at the gym is talkin' about it. Is it true?

THE NARRATOR
It totally isn't the truth. Nick almost kicked my ass, so don't think I'm a hero. I just tried to stop him. That's all. Please - tell everybody it wasn't like that. He's busy or something. He'll be back there. Don't treat him any different. He's just that same old Nick.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
Sure, sure, man, I got ya' covered. But I'm just sayin', it's all over town. You're the hottest topic flyin' around. People are sayin' Nick's little brother is a badass.

THE NARRATOR
Don't believe everything you hear.

He slaps KEVIN'S back and walks off toward the pizza place, coffee tight in his fist.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like the old telephone game. One kid says something, and by the end of the circle it's a different story. Most of the time I'd be glad to be the hero. Just not one that involved Nick.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

Spring light. They sit on the lumpy chocolate-colored couch. SIONNA opens a small red oriental box and lifts out a four-inch blue ceramic pipe with black swirls.

SIONNA
I've had this for ten or twelve years. I really don't sit around and smoke pot - I've been a little busy.

She pinches leaf into the bowl, lights it, holds it, then coughs the smoke out in little puffs.

SIONNA
Wow. That is pretty strong. I don't think I'll need much of that. Here, you go.

He inhales. The smoke lurches back out of him. He laughs and coughs.

THE NARRATOR
Yowsa. You're right. I don't need much of that either.

She crosses to the stereo. A Green Day CD; guitars fill the room. They sink back.

THE NARRATOR
What do you think this song means?

SIONNA
(twirling her hair)
I think it just means that none of us knows what will happen in life, but we have to make the best of things. In the end, I guess we'll see that everything is alright.

THE NARRATOR
That sounds about right. I think I'm stoned.

They both start laughing.

SIONNA
Sometimes I can see people's auras. Energy fields surround our bodies - we emanate the energy. Different people emit different colors, and sometimes, when I'm really lucky, I can see the colors.

THE NARRATOR
That's so cool! What color am I? Do you see it right now?

SIONNA
Not right now. But once or twice I saw it, and you are a beautiful color of blue. Like light blue. Blue is peaceful. Calm and friendly. It's loyalty, I suppose. You're a loyal person.

THE NARRATOR
(eyebrows up)
You think so?

SIONNA
All you have to do is realize you've been loyal to your Ma and your brother your entire life. That should be enough to prove it to you.

THE NARRATOR
What color do you think you are?

SIONNA
I can't see my own. But I feel like I'm usually blue - or purplish. Purple denotes faith, mystical qualities of some sort. I hate to sound pompous, but I think I might have mystical qualities of some sort.

THE NARRATOR
You have mystified me. Let's go outside, it's too nice to sit here on the couch.

She slings her cotton fiber purse over her left shoulder.

SIONNA
(wailing)
Ice cream! We must have ice cream!

THE NARRATOR
Totally.

EXT. DAIRYLAND CORNER - DAY

Three people at the tables out front. THE NARRATOR and SIONNA sit on a curved cement bench at a sticky white table, working on soft-serve chocolate waffle cones, saying "mmm" like children.

A shadow. BOB stands over them, hands in his pockets, flannel shirt untucked over a small beer belly.

BOB
Wow, man! How are you? Haven't seen you since the big brawl at Joe's.

THE NARRATOR
Oh - hey, man. Good to see you too. Bob, this is my girlfriend Sionna. Sionna, this is Bob.

SIONNA stands and shakes his hand.

SIONNA
Nice to meet you.

BOB
Nice to meet you too. Did you know your boyfriend is famous?

He digs a Marlboro out of his shirt pocket and lights it.

BOB
Your boyfriend here single-handedly conquered the BEAST, man!

THE NARRATOR
Knock it off, Bob.

BOB
You were right in Nick's face. You moved like Bruce Lee, dog! I never seen him that pissed off in his life. You stopped that shit dead. Everybody is bad-mouthing Nick now. Sayin' he's a pussy - 'scuse my language. Sayin' he's not the same Nick as he used to be.

THE NARRATOR
(heavy sigh)
Please stop those stupid rumors, man. Nick is still Nick. I'm just me. Enough said.

BOB
'Nuff said is right. Hey - tell Nick I want to go hang sometime.

THE NARRATOR
I haven't seen Nick. You mean, you haven't seen him either?

BOB
No, man. Not since that night. I figured he'd be at your Ma's, beggin' to the new king of the shit hill.

He laughs, slaps his thigh, ambles off down the street flicking ash at the traffic.

SIONNA
Wow. You have some pretty interesting friends.

THE NARRATOR
He's not my friend. He's Nick's friend. I just got stuck hanging out with him.

She laughs. He doesn't. He stares after BOB, cone melting in his hand.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
No friends, no gym, no phone call. I decided it was time to end this feud and find him.

INT. MA'S KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - MORNING

The smell of burning metal. MA sits in her chair in yesterday's clothes.

MA
I smell something burning. Can you smell it?

THE NARRATOR shuffles in wiping the sleep from his eyes, then hurries to the stove. The old coffeepot has boiled dry, metal scorching. He kills the burner, grabs the handle with an oven mitt and drops it into the sink. It hisses.

THE NARRATOR
Ma, how many times do I have to tell you NOT to try to cook anything? You're going to burn the damn building down!

MA
I did what?

He comes back to the living room. She has started to cry. He kneels and takes her hands in his.

THE NARRATOR
Now, now, Ma. Come on, don't cry. It's not that big of a deal, really.

MA
I can't remember anything anymore. I'm no good for anything.

THE NARRATOR
You're great, Ma. I love you.

She looks up at him with wet deer eyes.

MA
Nick doesn't love me. He never comes to see me anymore. What's wrong with him? Did I do something to make him mad?

THE NARRATOR
No. Of course not. I tell you what - I'll go check on him. I'll make sure everything is okay. Okay?

MA
Yeah. Sure. Just make sure Nick is alright. It's starting to worry me. My heart is a mess already. I feel it pounding a lot. Somedays I swear it's going to pound right out of my chest.

He hugs her. Over her shoulder, his eyes go flat with exhaustion.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Twelve-foot ceilings, ornate carved railing, original glass light fixture. THE NARRATOR taps at the door.

THE NARRATOR
Nick? Nick?

He knocks louder. A faint shuffling. The old door creaks open a slit, tarnished gold chain still hooked. Crazy brown hair, pox-marked face.

NICK
What do YOU want?

THE NARRATOR
Will you let me in, please?

NICK
What for?

THE NARRATOR
I think it's about time we talked this out. I mean, shit, Nick - Ma is worried about you.

Silence. NICK'S eyes squint.

NICK
I really don't need to talk to you, man. Why don't you just go home? Haven't you done enough damage?

THE NARRATOR
Nick. Please. Let me in, so we don't have to talk through a crack. Okay?

NICK
This better be good, man.

The door shuts, the chain rattles free, the door swings wide.

Inside looks like a tornado went through - papers, trash, empty food containers on the coffee table, lint and dropped clothing all over the rug. THE NARRATOR steps over a pile of magazines and clears a spot on the sofa to sit.

THE NARRATOR
Is everything okay?

NICK
What do YOU think, Moron?

THE NARRATOR just stares at him. NICK carries a cold cup of coffee into the kitchen, slams it in the microwave, comes back pacing like a man with only one aim.

NICK
You've ruined my life! I can't go anywhere, man. People are talkin' shit about me. I can't go to the gym. They're talkin' at work too - sayin' I'm a pussy, sayin' my little brother tried to kick my ass. All because of your big mouth. All because you tried to step in and be a hero. My reputation is dog shit. Thanks a lot, Bro. Thanks a lot.

THE NARRATOR
(slow, soft)
Nick, man - whoa. Slow down. I didn't do anything, man.

NICK snickers. The microwave beeps and neither of them moves.

THE NARRATOR
Look. I don't know exactly what happened that night. It's kind of a blur. I think I was just concerned you were going to hurt that guy for no reason. Anything I did just came out without thinking. I truly am sorry if you think I did something wrong. It's not what I intended to do. I just wanted to hang out, you know? Just hang out and enjoy the night. That's all, man. That's all.

He waits. NICK stands over him, breathing, saying nothing.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

NICK levels one finger at THE NARRATOR's face. Behind him, stacked dishes, unopened mail, the microwave still beeping.

NICK
Well, let me tell you something. You... YOU... are not the brother I knew, man. You're not! All I know is now you're some hot shit college kid with a hoity-toity girlfriend, and you know everything. Everything!

THE NARRATOR gets up off the sofa to face him.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, I'm not like that. I'm not. You just need to come over and see Ma and me more often. If you met Sionna, I know you would like her. Yes, she is smart. Yes, I am trying to dig my way out of my stupid job. But that's all, man. That's all there is to it. I don't know everything. I hardly know anything. That's the thing about going to school; you find out how much there is out there that you don't know.

He hangs his head, looks at his own folded hands.

THE NARRATOR
That's where I am. I'm working my ass off, and finding out how stupid I am. That's it, man.

NICK sits back against the couch. He looks around the room. A big sigh. Nobody moves.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I felt like a man on trial, waiting to hear if I had life without parole, or the death sentence.

NICK's eyes come up and bore into him.

NICK
I don't really give a shit how hard you're working. I don't give two shits about your girlfriend, and I don't give three shits about how dumb you are. But here is what I have to say. You are my little brother. I do care about you and Ma, and I will let it slide this time. But don't you ever, EVER, interrupt me in public again. You got that?

He stands up over THE NARRATOR. His voice climbs a rung.

NICK
I said, you got that?

THE NARRATOR
Yes, Nick. I got that. I've been trying to rectify it with your friends ever since I heard the truly fucked up story that's out there. I mean, really - do they think I could actually stop you? That's so dumb. It's just a stupid made up story.

NICK
It is a stupid made up story. And yes, you will stop it. And yes, you could never stop me from doing what I want in a million years.

THE NARRATOR
So, will you see Ma? She's dyin' to see you, man.

NICK
Yeah, I'll come over there tomorrow. Tell her I've been sick or somethin'. Don't tell her about any of this shit. She doesn't know about any of this shit, right?

His forehead creases down, suspicious.

THE NARRATOR
Of course not. You know Ma. Hell, she cried today because she forgot the burner was on in the kitchen. Took me a half hour to calm her down. She's losing her memory big-time.

NICK
I know, I know. She'll probably have to go to an old folks home one of these days. It's the pits. I'm just tellin' you, man - you better be giving her her meds, or else she's going to end up in the damn hospital.

THE NARRATOR
Absolutely.

He moves to the door.

THE NARRATOR
Thanks, Nick. It means a lot to me that you can forgive me. We'll see you tomorrow then?

NICK
Yeah, yeah, see you tomorrow.

NICK opens the door. THE NARRATOR steps out. The door shuts. On this side of it, the sound of papers and dishes being shoved around.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was still pissed off. But at least I would have some help with Ma.

INT. MA'S KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - EVENING

MA sits in her chair, laughing - gentle, deep, heartfelt. SIONNA sits close, listening. THE NARRATOR stops in the doorway, keys still in his hand.

MA
On Halloween, we would watch from the bushes, and my brothers would tip those outhouses over!

SIONNA
Sounds like you were a bunch of troublemakers.

MA
We were just plain hoodlums! But I didn't have a care in the world. It really was a nice place to grow up. My parents were good people, hardworking, and regular members of the church.

She goes still. Then looks up at THE NARRATOR the way a child looks at a parent.

MA
Why don't we go to church anymore?

THE NARRATOR
You always say you don't want to, Ma. Do you want to? I'll take you if you want to.

MA
Yes, I do. That would be fine. Let's go next Sunday. It's been too long.

THE NARRATOR
Okay, Ma. We'll go.

He pictures something and laughs out loud in spite of himself. SIONNA gives him a suspicious look.

SIONNA
What's so funny?

THE NARRATOR
Oh, nothin'. Just thinking about church and Nick, and the combination.

SIONNA shakes her head and goes to the kitchen, cutting peach pie, warming slices, dropping vanilla ice cream on top. A knock at the door - then the door opens by itself. NICK walks in, wild hair, work jacket.

THE NARRATOR
Oh, hey. Um, hi. How are you doing?

MA
Oh my God! It's you, Nick! I thought we would never see you again. Where have you been hiding?

She leans forward for her kiss on the cheek.

NICK
Oh, you know. Just busy working and crap like that. How ya doin' Ma? This one been treatin' you all right?

MA
Silly you. Of course, of course. We are all just fine.

SIONNA comes in with two plates of pie, sets them on the table, wipes her hands on her jeans, and puts out her hand. NICK looks at it like it is attached to an alien being.

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you, I've heard so much about you.

NICK
Uh, yeah. Likewise.

He shakes it, barely.

SIONNA
You're just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. I can't eat that crap. Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

SIONNA
Alright, that's fine. Let me know if you want anything. Don't want to make you feel left out.

NICK
Riiight.

Silence. Only forks scraping lightly on plates. NICK looks SIONNA up and down.

NICK
So, I hear you're a university lady.

SIONNA
(chewing, polite)
That's right.

NICK
What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
Well, that's a tough one. I have a major in English, and will have a minor in Philosophy, so I am thinking college professor - if I can get my student teaching done and write a decent thesis.

NICK
Well, professor, I hope the best for ya. It's tough out there, and, like I was tellin' my kid brother here, sometimes all that school just doesn't pay off. So I hope you can break through that glass ceiling, or whatever. Because, you know, it's even harder for a woman to teach at that level. Or so I've heard.

THE NARRATOR's fork stops halfway to his mouth. SIONNA sets her plate lightly on the table.

SIONNA
Well, Nick, I have to disagree with you there. In the last forty years, bachelor's degrees earned by female students have risen from about thirty-five to fifty-five percent. There are more females in college today than males - we make up about sixty percent of all undergraduate students. And many of those women are schooling part-time, juggling the demands of family and work. In my line of thinking, I just don't see how the future of education cannot be significantly changed for the better by the presence of more female professors. You can't keep a good woman down, so to speak.

She smiles at him, picks up her plate, and rises to collect MA's.

MA
(head tilted)
Oh my.

NICK
Well, if you ask me, that might be one of the reasons this country is heading down the flippin' tubes. The women in this country can't stay home and take care of their kids.

He glances at MA.

NICK
No disrespect, Ma. I know you had no choice.

SIONNA
You might be right in saying that children need the guidance of a mother figure. And you may be right that not only your mother, but many other women today, have no choice in the matter. Women cannot afford to stay at home and "take care of kids," as you say. But I would have to disagree with you that this is purely the reason for a failing society. Women bring different ways of thinking to the table. Diversity helps us learn. After all, we are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

She walks into the kitchen before he can get another word out. NICK sits stunned. THE NARRATOR fights down a grin.

THE NARRATOR
Well. Who would have known all that stuff? Gee, Nick, I'm really glad you came to check on Ma. Aren't you glad, Ma?

MA
Oh, yes, yes. I've missed you, dear.

She pats his hand, then shows him the little plastic pill containers on the side table.

MA
See, the kids have put all my pills into these little containers so I won't get confused. Thanks to Sionna, everything is straightening out nicely.

NICK stands. He gives MA another peck on the cheek. His smile is thin and snake-like.

NICK
Well, I guess I better get going. Someone in this room has a job they have to go to tomorrow. Sounds like you two have it all figured out. With help, of course, from Doctor Sionna.

SIONNA comes back in, drying her hands.

SIONNA
Oh gosh, are you leaving already? We hardly had a chance to talk at all.

NICK
Um, yeah. Well, I'm sure I'll see you around. Take care of this numbskull.

He grabs THE NARRATOR's shoulders and jiggles him around. His face is red, his hair dancing around like blazes. SIONNA's face is a still, calm pool of water.

THE NARRATOR opens the door. On the threshold, NICK lightly takes a fistful of his shirt and pulls his face in close.

NICK
(whisper)
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, you know I will.

NICK goes off down the hallway, hand already digging in his pocket for a cigarette.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
More and more, his presence made me sick. Like I couldn't smile with him around. Like I didn't need him anymore.

INT. UNIVERSITY LIBRARY - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR at a long table under a green lamp, still in his pizza joint shirt. Open books: the Peloponnesian Wars, Greek mythology.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So, just like A Tale of Two Cities, it was the best of times, and it was the worst of times. I was keeping a low profile. She never spoke about him, but he always had a snide remark about her.

He turns a page. A plate of ATHENA, armored, spear in hand.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick came around more to check on Ma. He even started laying out her pills and bringing her treats to eat. Looking back, his behavior was a little over the top. She began to trust him again, doting on him like she used to. Nick was in - and I was out.

He turns another page. A dark woodcut of a falling titan.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Athena. Goddess of intelligence and skill. A peaceful warrior, born fully armored and ready for battle. Not that Sionna looked for battles.

His pencil taps once and stops on a single word: hubristic.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was more like this god named Menoetius. Hubristic. Exceedingly prideful and impetuous to the very end. Was I in the middle of some sort of tragic Greek play? I always thought I loved Nick. Then I met Sionna, and it threw everything into a different light. Love wasn't competitive, or demeaning.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - DAY

SIONNA types at the computer, books open around her. THE NARRATOR comes in, drops his backpack, flops down beside her.

THE NARRATOR
Class got cancelled. Pizza joint's dead. I'm a free man for one whole afternoon.

SIONNA
(still typing)
Creation stories. Every religion has one, and they all rhyme. That's the thing - the more you look into some things, the less you believe any of it can be true.

He picks up one of her books, turns it over.

THE NARRATOR
Not that long ago, man had a hard time grasping the idea that the earth was round, or that it revolved around the sun. Now it seems so stupid. What if what we believe right now seems ludicrous in another hundred years?

SIONNA
Maybe God is just energy. The energy is in all of us, and in everything. It existed before time as we know it, and it will go on forever.

THE NARRATOR
Forever. I can't even wrap my head around forever. Does energy give a flying rat's ass if I live or die? I don't know. But down deep inside, I think if we all put our energy into something - like prayer - it has to work. Energy has power. So all together, it has to increase that power.

She stops typing and looks at him. Outside, the light shifts across the wall.

INT. MA'S KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - DAY

Saturday afternoon. NICK stands in the middle of the room with his car keys, like a man delivering the weather.

NICK
I'll take her to St. Patrick's. Four o'clock mass.

THE NARRATOR's jaw comes open. MA flutters up out of her chair, patting her hair.

MA
What should I wear?

THE NARRATOR
Anything, Ma. God sees you all the time anyway.

MA
Well. That's true.

She takes NICK's arm and shuffles out with him. The door closes. THE NARRATOR goes to the window.

Below, NICK's Pontiac pulls out of the lot, a thread of cigarette smoke trailing from the driver's window.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the past, I would have just been relieved that somebody else was doing something. Now I had to question Nick's actions.

The brake lights flare at the corner, then the car is gone.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
You might think I'm being paranoid, but you don't know Nick like I do. You don't just go from tough guy, leader of the pack, scary mother, to pet your Mom's head and escort her to church. What is that?

He stays at the glass, one hand flat on the frame.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He is our fearless leader. Our warrior. Our Viking hero. He is also one mean son of a bitch. Down deep, he is us. What would I be, without Nick?

I/E. SIONNA'S TOYOTA - COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

An old Toyota hatchback climbs and drops over the soft hills of Eastern Wisconsin. Bright blue sky, a few cumulus clouds, pines and maples in full summer green. SIONNA drives, one hand easy on the wheel. THE NARRATOR rides shotgun, window down.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
First week of summer break, and I was actually taking a break for once. Ma was in the hands of Nick the chameleon, and we were on our way to meet Sionna's parents. Mushroom and Ginger.

A deer dashes along the forest floor at the shoulder and is gone.

THE NARRATOR
They actually like being called Mushroom and Ginger? You're sure?

SIONNA
Yep. Just call them Mushroom and Ginger. They've gone by that since the sixties. They wouldn't even respond to Robert and Margery.

THE NARRATOR
Oh. So they do have real names.

SIONNA
Yes. They were raised right here in the good ol' Midwest, christened with proper names and everything.

THE NARRATOR
So how'd that go over with their own parents?

SIONNA
My father's parents had five other kids. I think they just mentally divorced him. I saw them once at the farm. A lot of yelling, and my grandmother crying. Her hair piled up in a beehive, a cheap looking blue polyester dress. She looked at me through the screen door with tears streaming down her face. They're both dead now.

She keeps her eyes on the road, moving the wheel in a sleepy manner.

SIONNA
Mom's parents tried really hard to be in our lives when I was little. But Grandma and Grandpa just didn't get tofu. Or making yogurt, unpasteurized milk, drumming around an outside fire, you know.

She starts to chuckle.

SIONNA
They also discouraged any belief in the tooth fairy, the Easter bunny, or Santa Claus. Elves - that was possible. Wood nymphs, the spiritual life of plants, trees and water - all okay. I guess you could say I never got my hopes up unnecessarily.

THE NARRATOR
So. Your parents are pagans.

SIONNA
(deciding)
Yeah. I guess that's as close as we're going to get.

The road tips them over another green hill.

INT. SIONNA'S LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Textbooks, highlighters and an open bag of chips on the rug. THE NARRATOR - mid-twenties, thrift-store flannel, hands thickened from ovens - sits against the couch. SIONNA - long brown hair, wool cardigan - is cross-legged beside him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This is Murphy's Law. Whatever you think is going to happen is not what happens. Basically, it's pure irony. Just when you think everything is going your way, man, suddenly, it isn't.

She pushes the chips at him with her foot.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It's better for me to feel shitty and have everything turn out better than expected, than to wake up all happy and have things go to shit. And that's exactly what happened. Everything went to shit.

SIONNA
Wow, your brother is sure suddenly into taking care of your mother, isn't he?

She looks at him with a quizzical nod of the head.

THE NARRATOR
I can hardly remember the last time I made dinner over there. He's always taking her out or bringing something over for her. Does seem kind of weird, doesn't it?

SIONNA
Kind of.

THE NARRATOR
He'll be back to neglecting her in no time. Then I'll have to start hanging out there more. I'm sorry for all this. I wish we didn't have to constantly worry about her. I know it's a drag.

She looks upset, reaches up and pats the side of his head.

SIONNA
Oh no, no, it's not like that at all. I can only hope I have a son or daughter like you to take care of me when I'm old. Isn't that one of the reasons we have children? To help us in our golden years?

THE NARRATOR
Well, I suppose. It just feels kind of like we're indentured servants or something.

SIONNA
(laughing at him)
Oh my god, you're reading too much. I think of it as part of the circle of life. First they feed us and change our diapers. They teach us everything we know to become independent people. Then we're all grown up and at the same level. Then finally they start to trend downwards and we're helping them eat and possibly changing diapers.

She gets a faraway look in her eyes.

THE NARRATOR
Right, well, I don't have anyone around yet to change my diapers. Unless you'd like to sign up, of course.

SIONNA
No, that's okay.

THE NARRATOR
Do you want to have children someday?

SIONNA
To be honest, I'm too busy right now to think about it. I'm going on thirty though, so I guess I better get my shit together if I'm going to. I really want to be ready. I want to honestly have lots of time to devote to taking care of another human. I think that's the least I can do.

THE NARRATOR
I agree. Look at all the people that don't have time, and the kids they end up with.

SIONNA
What about you? Do you want kids?

THE NARRATOR
I suppose. I do need someone to change those diapers.

She leans up and hits him on the arm. They go back to their books.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a great night. It was a normal night. It was the last normal night.

INT. MA'S KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - DAY

September light through thin curtains. THE NARRATOR lets himself in and stops. Complete quiet and stillness. Ma's chair sits empty, the afghan folded over the arm. No purse. No coat.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Where the hell would she be at nine a.m.? Nick took her to church last night. He was supposed to bring her back and make sure she had dinner.

On the phone table the answering machine light blinks red. He presses the button.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. Mr. Olson, once again, this is Officer Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

His hand stays on the machine.

PATRICIA OWENS (V.O.)
Hello Mr. Olson, this is Patricia Owens, one of the nurses at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

He rewinds the tape, copies the number onto the back of an envelope, dials.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Yes, Mr. Olson. As a matter of fact, we stopped by your mother's apartment early this morning but apparently missed you. Would you be able to come down here to the station so we could have a word with you?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, I guess. What's going on? Is Ma okay? I wasn't here last night. I have no idea where she is.

He rubs his temples. Cold sweat on his forehead.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Yes, we know, Mr. Olson. Everything will be okay. We just think it best if you came in to talk directly to us.

THE NARRATOR
Okay. Sure. I'll be there in a few minutes.

He hangs up, dials again, listens to it ring and ring, then drops the receiver and runs out.

EXT. POLICE STATION - CENTRAL AND 3RD - DAY

An ancient stone building. Lion heads and stars carved into the thick stone around the windows. A metal plaque beside the door: 1898.

THE NARRATOR pedals up the sidewalk far too fast. A kid on a skateboard bails onto the gravel shoulder, shouting after him. He never turns his head. He drops the bike against the steps and goes in.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

A wall of sound. People scattered around waiting. A brunette officer behind the chest-high counter takes his name and gives him a concerned, sorry look, then waves him back to a chair.

OFFICER DUPUIS - big, graying hair, thin mustache, pressed uniform - crosses to him and sits on the edge of a desk.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Thanks for coming down to the station, Mr. Olson. It seems that there has been a serious accident involving your brother, Mr. Nicholas Olson, and your mother, Mrs. Olivia Olson. Last night about eight p.m. they were hit broadside going through the intersection of Werner and Lowe. I regret to tell you that your brother is in serious condition at Frodo, and your mother passed away at the scene.

The room's noise seems to drain away. THE NARRATOR sits perfectly still.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Her body is at Sacred Heart Funeral Home. I know this is a huge shock to you. We will help you with whatever we can.

THE NARRATOR
Can I have some water? Can I call someone? Can I have a ride to the hospital?

A styrofoam cup of cold water shakes in his hands.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Toxicology is still pending. There's a possibility your brother had been drinking.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Really? Drunk and in church. So that's how he's been putting up with everything. He just gets plastered and finishes his chores. Way to go, Nick.

He picks up the desk phone, dials, waits. A machine answers. He can't get a full sentence out. He sets the receiver down.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

People in scrubs move fast in every direction. Beeping from all sides. The air smells of urine and chemicals. PATRICIA OWENS - forties, ponytail, stethoscope - sits him down in the corridor.

PATRICIA OWENS
He's unconscious for now, but stable. His right lung was punctured and his right femur is broken. They'll take him to surgery later to put a rod in it. He has a lot of cuts from the windshield.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Pontiac was totaled. They had to pull Ma out with the jaws of life. She never knew it hit her. Thank god. At least she'd just been to mass.

Across the hall, through the high windows, the top of NICK's head - erratic, fire-colored hair poking straight up, a white bandage framing a face we can't see. Wires and machines crowd the bed.

PATRICIA OWENS
Are you up to seeing him?

THE NARRATOR
No. I'm really not up to seeing him. Sorry.

He turns around and walks out.

INT. MA'S KITCHEN AND LIVING ROOM - DAY

He stands in the doorway staring at Ma's chair. Pill bottles lined up on the kitchen counter.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She wouldn't be sitting there anymore. She wouldn't be using her afghan, walking into the kitchen to take her goddamn pills. She was dead. And Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

He goes down onto the floor, holding his head, water falling on the linoleum, pulling at his own hair.

THE NARRATOR
Die, you fucking jackass. Die. Die.

Later. Dusk in the windows. SIONNA sits on the floor with him, his head against her, patting his hair.

SIONNA
Just let it out. Let it out. We'll figure it all out. Don't worry.

The phone rings. She reaches up, takes the receiver, listens, pulls a notepad toward her.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Do you want the top of the line casket with engraved flowers on the lid? The pink chiffon lining? The vault to keep out creepy crawlers while she lays in her tomb six feet under? One day you're talking to her, the next day you're putting her in a box.

Sionna writes. Her hand stays on his knee.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sionna was the voice of reason in a lot of these decisions. A moderately priced casket. A plot in the Catholic cemetery. A priest for the mass.

EXT. CATHOLIC CEMETERY - GRAVESIDE - DAY

A small crowd around a light maple-colored casket. Old neighbors from the apartment building, a few surviving coworkers, FATHER JOSEPH with his book open. SIONNA holds THE NARRATOR's arm.

BOB - lanky, leather jacket, pupils huge - sidles up, solemn as a bad actor.

BOB
I'm checking on Nick all the time, man. I'll watch over his apartment.

THE NARRATOR
Good.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was the only son there. The oldest one would not be able to attend his own mother's funeral. Rightly so. Since he killed her.

He takes a handful of dirt and lets it go. It hits the lid with a muted thunking sound.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

NICK - late thirties, thick neck, football build gone soft, right leg in a cast, hospital gown, two days of stubble - lies propped on the pillows, staring at the ceiling tiles.

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all her fault. She really is the reason this is all going down like this. I had lots and lots of time to think while I laid there in that stupid hole they call a hospital.

A doctor leans in, taps the chart, says something about his lungs, goes. A second doctor leans in, says something about the leg, writes a different order, goes. A nurse hangs a fresh bag on the pole without looking at him.

NICK (V.O.)
What a bunch of quacks. They all should get their licenses taken away. I don't think one of them knows their ass from a hole in the ground.

OFFICER DUPUIS stands at the foot of the bed, notepad open.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your blood alcohol was over the limit, Mr. Olson. You should have known better than to get behind that wheel. Your mother was the victim here.

NICK
What about me?

Dupuis writes. Says nothing. Nick picks at the tape on the back of his hand.

NICK (V.O.)
I mean, I know she's dead. But what about my pain and suffering? Like it

INT. BELLTOWN APARTMENT BEDROOM - NIGHT

A dream. Gray glass water beyond a window that is not a window.

EXT. FERRY UPPER DECK - DAY

THE NARRATOR - late thirties now, broad-shouldered, close-cropped brown hair graying at the temples, gray hooded rain jacket - climbs punched-metal stairs, one hand on the cold round rail. Coffee in one fist, a burrito in shiny silver foil in the other.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The heat of it radiates into my palm. I feel satisfied knowing it will soon warm my empty stomach.

The coffee is gone. He walks the deck holding the hand of a SMALL BOY, about two years old, on his left. The center of the floor opens into a square, a banister all the way around. Far below, the hard wooden floor of the lower level.

The boy is violently thrust over the banister.

The Narrator's left hand snaps out and catches a small, plump ankle between first finger and thumb. The boy hangs limp, upside down. Silent.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Time slows. If I let go, he falls to his death on the wooden floor far below us.

The banister cuts into his waist. His right hand stays clamped to the rail, useless. Finger and thumb slide, millimeter by millimeter, working around the limb without changing the pressure.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
But the boy is me. I am my only savior.

INT. BELLTOWN APARTMENT BEDROOM - NIGHT

With a jolt, he is awake. Sweat beading on his forehead. Rain ticks against the glass.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Can I pull him up without falling myself?

EXT. PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Mist

FADE OUT.

THE END
